preConditionHook:
// THE RESTING LIMIT ORDER UNDER THE PRIOR BAR. Price traded at least
// dipTicks below the prior bar's low (mirrored: at least that far above the
// prior bar's high) -- the with-trend buy that bulls keep resting at or below
// the prior bar's low all through a bull channel, and the same order that fades
// a countertrend setup bar in a trend.
//
// ONE RULE, TWO MERGED KEYS. DipBelowPriorBarLowByTicks (same slice, R28-25 and
// R32-03) is this identical expression with the input spelled offsetTicks:
// R28-25 rests the buy four ticks below the bear signal bar's low (two or three
// ticks above where the short scalpers take profit, six ticks down), R32-03 buys
// at the failing setup bar's extreme, which is this rule at 0. Seven statements
// across both books, one parameter. Offsets seen: 0 (T04-11, T06-30, T21-16,
// R32-03), 1 to 3 (T15-18 p273), 4 (R28-25). Default 1, the most supported
// reading.
//
// NOT STRICT: at dipTicks = 0 the test is low[0] <= low[1], which is
// LaggedHigherHigh at lookback 0 flipped only if that rule's strict test is
// loosened -- the two disagree on a bar whose low exactly equals the prior low,
// and the default of 1 tick separates them outright.
//
// Every source wraps this in a channel or strong-trend context (TightChannel,
// ChannelSlopeSteep, the always-in read); the entry pane ANDs, so the context is
// a separate placement and only the bar test lives here. Kept apart from
// ShallowDipBelowPriorBarLow, which bounds the dip from ABOVE instead of below
// and fires on a disjoint set of bars whenever both bounds bite.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Every scratch value is a localVariables entry,
// never a hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
